ैं—उनके घरबार, बालबच्चे या थोड़ी बहुत बैंक-बचत हो जाती है वे फलते-फूलते प्रतीत होते हैं\nकिन्तु बाद में वे शीघ्र ही उसी तरह शुष्क हो जाते हैं जिस तरह छोटी छोटी नदियाँ तथा पोखर सूख",
      "textEn": "150\n\nShrutva—Having heard; Parjanya—Of rain clouds; Ninadam—Echo; Mandukah—Frogs; Sasrujuh—Having taken out; Girah—His own\nSound; Tushnim—In silence; Shayanah—Lying down; Prak—Before; Yadvat—As; Brahmanah—Brahmin students; Niyama-\nAtyaye—By completing the morning rituals. Those frogs that had been silent until now, after hearing the thunder of the rainy season clouds, suddenly\nstarted to trill as if the Brahmin students, who perform morning rituals with a calm demeanor, by their guru\nwere about to recite their lesson upon being called. Meaning: Srila Prabhupada has commented on this in this way: “After the first rain, when there is thunder in the clouds,\nall the frogs start croaking as if suddenly the students have started reading their lesson. Students usually wake up early in the morning. But they do not wake up voluntarily, but only when the bell rings in the temple or\ncultural institution.\nThey immediately get up by the order of the spiritual guru.\nAnd after performing the morning rituals, they sit down to study the Vedas or chant the Vedic mantras.\nThey sit down. Similarly, in the darkness of Kali Yuga, every person keeps sleeping, but as soon as a great teacher\ncalls out, every person starts studying the Vedas to attain true knowledge.”\n\nThose who are on the path of the unattainable, the small streams dry up. The body, as it were, is independent, and so is its wealth of body-possessions. 10. Meaning of the word\n\nAasan—became; Utpat-gaminyah—from their respective paths; Kshudra—inconsequent; Nadyah—rivers; Anushushyati—drying up;\nPunasah—of a human; Yatha—as; Asvatantasya—of one who is not independent; Deha—body;\nDravina—physical property; Sampadah—and wealth. With the arrival of the rainy season, the dried-up small rivers started to increase and, leaving their original paths,\nstarted flowing here and there, as if the body, property, and wealth of a person controlled by the senses.\nMeaning: Srila Prabhupada's commentary is as follows: “In the rainy season, many ponds, lakes, and small\nsmall rivers are filled with water; otherwise, they remain dry during the remaining period of the year. Similarly,\nphysicalistic people are dry, but sometimes when they are in so-called luxurious situations,\ntheir households, children, or a little bit of bank savings are there, they seem to flourish,\nbut later they quickly become as dry as small rivers and ponds dry up"
